<html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><style>@page {size:A4; margin:1in} body{font-family:"Times New Roman";font-size:12pt;line-height:1.5} h1{font-size:16pt;color:#17365d;page-break-before:always} h2{font-size:13pt;color:#17365d} p{text-align:justify} .cover{text-align:center;padding-top:90px} .cover p{text-align:center;margin:28px} table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%} td{border:1px solid #777;padding:6px} tr:first-child{font-weight:bold;background:#dce6f1} pre{font-family:Consolas,monospace;font-size:9pt;background:#f2f4f7;padding:10px;white-space:pre-wrap}</style></head><body><div class="cover"><h1>JOBPILOT</h1><h2>INTELLIGENT JOB DISCOVERY AND APPLICATION ASSISTANCE</h2><p>MINI PROJECT REPORT / SYNOPSIS</p><p>Submitted in partial fulfilment of the requirements for the award of<br><b>Bachelor of Technology in Computer Science &amp; Design</b></p><p>Submitted by: [Name and Roll Number]<br>Under the guidance of: [Guide Name and Designation]</p><p>Department of Computer Science &amp; Design<br>R.R. Institute of Modern Technology, Lucknow<br>Affiliated to Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam Technical University<br>College Code: 361</p><p>[Month] 2026–27</p></div><h1>Institute and Department: Vision and Mission</h1><p>Institute vision: To provide high-quality education and develop competent, ethical and confident professionals who contribute to society and national development. Institute mission: To maintain a dynamic, collaborative learning environment and develop analytical and technical competence.</p><p>Department vision: To develop technically competent professionals with professional, ethical and moral values. Department mission: To provide current knowledge, maintain an effective learning environment, support careers and research, and encourage lifelong learning and professional ethics.</p><h1>Programme Educational Objectives and Specific Outcomes</h1><p>PEOs: Build a foundation in computer science and design; develop communication and professional skills; and prepare graduates for industry, higher education and research. PSOs: Apply algorithms, data science, networks, security and web technologies to practical problems; and use multidisciplinary approaches to design solutions for industry and society.</p><h1>Programme and Course Outcomes</h1><p>The project supports engineering knowledge, problem analysis, solution design, investigation, modern tool usage, societal awareness, ethics, teamwork, communication, project management and lifelong learning (PO1–PO11). Course outcomes include problem formulation, literature review, planning, implementation, testing, interpretation and professional reporting (CO1–CO5).</p><h1>Acknowledgement</h1><p>We express our gratitude to our guide [Guide Name], the Department of Computer Science &amp; Design, and R.R. Institute of Modern Technology for their guidance and support. We also thank all those who contributed to this project.</p><h1>Preface</h1><p>This report presents JobPilot, a system for discovering jobs and assisting applicants through profile-based matching and supervised application workflows. It describes the design, algorithms, requirements, testing approach and proposed enhancements.</p><h1>Contents</h1><p>1 Introduction • 2 Feasibility Study • 3 Requirement and Analysis • 4 Survey of Technology • 5 Preliminary Module Description • 6 System Design • 7 Detailed Design • 8 Testing Techniques • 9 Implementation and Maintenance • 10 Limitations, Future Scope and Enhancements • 11 References and Bibliography • Appendix</p><h1>Chapter 1: Introduction</h1><h2>1.1 Background</h2><p>Job seekers often search multiple career sites, repeat the same information in application forms and struggle to track their progress. JobPilot brings job discovery, profile management, application assistance and status tracking into one interface. Source adapters collect listings from supported public job boards and normalize them for search and recommendations.</p><h2>1.2 Objectives</h2><p>Provide a searchable job catalogue; rank opportunities using profile and job attributes; reduce repeated form entry; obtain user input for unknown required answers; present answers for review; and maintain an application timeline.</p><h2>1.3 Purpose and Scope</h2><p>The system supports applicants and administrators. Applicants manage profiles and résumés, browse jobs and review applications. Administrators manage job sources, workflows and operational settings. Browser workflows support selected applicant tracking systems and a generic fallback. Source availability and site-specific behaviour determine coverage.</p><h2>1.4 Limitations</h2><p>External websites change independently, some portals require dedicated workflows, and challenges may require human interaction. Automated confirmation is not always conclusive. Recommendations depend on profile quality and available job data.</p><h1>Chapter 2: Feasibility Study</h1><h2>2.1 Economic Feasibility</h2><p>Open-source frameworks and a modular architecture reduce initial licensing costs. Hosting, browser execution, storage, support and optional AI inference contribute to recurring cost. Actual expenditure depends on traffic and deployment choices.</p><h2>2.2 Technical Feasibility</h2><p>A React-based interface, Node.js services, PostgreSQL, Redis and browser automation provide a practical technology base. Service queues separate long-running browser tasks from interactive requests. Integration feasibility depends on third-party site behaviour and access.</p><h2>2.3 Behavioral Feasibility</h2><p>A single interface and reusable profile reduce repetitive work. User review and requests for missing answers retain applicant control. Adoption depends on clear consent, understandable status messages and accessible interfaces.</p><h1>Chapter 3: Requirement and Analysis</h1><h2>3.1 Problem Definition</h2><p>Design a system that collects and normalizes job information, identifies relevant opportunities and assists with applications while making user approval and uncertainty visible.</p><h2>3.2 Planning and Scheduling</h2><pre>Start → Requirements → Design → Development → Integration → Testing → Review → Maintenance

Activity                 W1  W2  W3  W4  W5  W6
Requirements             ██  ██
Architecture                 ██  ██
Interface and services           ██  ██  ██
Integration                          ██  ██
Testing and documentation                ██  ██</pre><h2>3.3 Hardware and Software Requirements</h2><table><tr><td>Category</td><td>Requirement</td></tr><tr><td>Development machine</td><td>Modern multicore processor, 8 GB RAM recommended, sufficient storage and internet access</td></tr><tr><td>Client</td><td>Modern browser; Android build supported through Capacitor</td></tr><tr><td>Server software</td><td>Node.js, PostgreSQL, Redis and Chromium</td></tr><tr><td>Development tools</td><td>Code editor, Git, npm and browser developer tools</td></tr></table><h2>3.4 SDLC Model</h2><p>An iterative SDLC is appropriate: collect requirements, design a small feature, implement, test, review feedback and repeat. Each iteration can improve a source adapter or application workflow without redesigning the entire system.</p><h1>Chapter 4: Survey of Technology</h1><h2>4.1 Previous Work</h2><p>Conventional job boards offer search and application links, while applicant tracking systems host employer-specific forms. JobPilot combines normalized listings with a reusable profile and review-oriented workflow. Integration is constrained by the interfaces and policies of each external service.</p><h2>4.2 Technology Used</h2><table><tr><td>Layer</td><td>Technology and purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Front end</td><td>React, TypeScript and Vite; Capacitor for Android packaging</td></tr><tr><td>Back end</td><td>Node.js, TypeScript and Fastify services</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>PostgreSQL for structured records; Redis and BullMQ for queues and coordination</td></tr><tr><td>Automation</td><td>Playwright and Chromium for supported browser workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Planned extension</td><td>Java for portable algorithm prototypes and potential service integration</td></tr></table><h2>4.3 IDE, APIs and Tools</h2><p>Development tools include a code editor, Git, npm, browser developer tools and test runners. Public job board APIs supply listings; application services expose HTTP endpoints. The source adapters include Greenhouse, Lever and Ashby among others.</p><h1>Chapter 5: Preliminary Module Description</h1><table><tr><td>Module</td><td>Responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>User and profile</td><td>Authentication, profile data, résumé and preferences</td></tr><tr><td>Job ingestion</td><td>Poll sources, normalize records and update job status</td></tr><tr><td>Search and matching</td><td>Filter and rank active jobs</td></tr><tr><td>Application execution</td><td>Choose workflow, fill fields, request answers and present review</td></tr><tr><td>Tracking</td><td>Store application status and events</td></tr><tr><td>Administration</td><td>Manage sources, workflows, workers and settings</td></tr></table><h1>Chapter 6: System Design</h1><h2>6.1 Architecture and Data Flow</h2><pre>Applicant → Web / Android interface → API service
                                         ↓
             Job boards → Ingestion → PostgreSQL ← Application records
                                         ↕
                                    Redis / queues
                                         ↓
                             Worker → Browser → Employer portal
                                         ↕
                               Review and user responses</pre><p>DFD level 0: The applicant exchanges profile, search and review information with JobPilot; the system exchanges job and application information with external job boards and employer portals.</p><pre>DFD level 1:
Applicant → [1. Profile] → Profile store
Applicant → [2. Search and match] ← Job store ← [3. Ingestion] ← Job boards
Applicant → [4. Application] ↔ Application store
[4. Application] ↔ Employer portal; [4. Application] ↔ Applicant review</pre><pre>DFD level 2 — application process:
Select job → Resolve workflow → Queue run → Scan form → Resolve answers
→ Ask for missing answers → Review → Submit → Record outcome</pre><pre>DFD level 3 — answer resolution:
Form field → Profile lookup → Answer memory lookup → User question
                                      ↓
                            Validate option → Review</pre><h2>6.2 Entity Relationship Diagram</h2><pre>USER 1 ─── N APPLICATION N ─── 1 JOB
USER 1 ─── 1 PROFILE
USER 1 ─── N ANSWER
JOB BOARD 1 ─── N JOB
WORKFLOW 1 ─── N APPLICATION (logical association)</pre><h2>6.3 Data Structures and Database</h2><table><tr><td>Entity</td><td>Representative attributes</td></tr><tr><td>User</td><td>id, account identity</td></tr><tr><td>Profile</td><td>user_id, contact information, skills and preferences</td></tr><tr><td>Job</td><td>id, source, title, location, status</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>id, user_id, job_id, status, timestamps</td></tr><tr><td>Answer</td><td>user_id, question, saved answer</td></tr><tr><td>Job board</td><td>source type, token, configuration</td></tr><tr><td>Workflow</td><td>name, patterns, ordered actions</td></tr></table><p>This is a conceptual schema; actual migrations define the authoritative columns and constraints.</p><h1>Chapter 7: Detailed Design</h1><h2>7.1 Inputs and Outputs</h2><p>Inputs include profile information, résumé, search filters, job selection and review responses. Outputs include ranked job cards, application status, prompts for missing answers and a timeline. Project screenshots should be inserted here after capturing the running interface: profile, job search, review and application history.</p><h2>7.2 Validation Checks</h2><p>Validate required inputs, session ownership, URL and field formats, file constraints and valid choices. Before submission, compare current form values with approved values and surface site validation errors. An uncertain submission must remain distinguishable from a confirmed success.</p><h2>7.3 Data Structures and Algorithms</h2><p>Job filtering traverses candidate jobs in O(n); sorting k matches takes O(k log k). Hash sets support average O(1) skill membership checks. A queue coordinates asynchronous tasks; database indexes support lookups. Workflow execution processes an ordered sequence of steps. These are conceptual complexities; actual performance depends on queries, indexing and data distribution.</p><h2>7.4 Illustrative Java Code — Skill Overlap</h2><p>The following Java prototype illustrates a planned matching component. It is an example algorithm, not a listing of the existing service implementation.</p><pre>import java.util.*;

class SkillMatcher {
    static double score(Set&lt;String&gt; user, Set&lt;String&gt; job) {
        if (job.isEmpty()) return 0.0;
        int matches = 0;
        for (String skill : job) {
            if (user.contains(skill)) matches++;
        }
        return (double) matches / job.size();
    }
}</pre><p>With hash sets, the loop has expected O(m) time for m job skills and O(1) extra space, excluding the input sets. Production ranking can also account for role, location, seniority and authorization.</p><h1>Chapter 8: Testing Techniques</h1><table><tr><td>Technique</td><td>Example</td></tr><tr><td>Unit testing</td><td>Verify normalization, filtering and field validation</td></tr><tr><td>Integration testing</td><td>Check API, database and queue interactions</td></tr><tr><td>System testing</td><td>Exercise search, review and application tracking</td></tr><tr><td>Browser workflow testing</td><td>Test supported forms and user review</td></tr><tr><td>Regression testing</td><td>Repeat representative flows after workflow changes</td></tr><tr><td>Acceptance testing</td><td>Confirm usability and expected user outcomes</td></tr></table><p>Tests should use controlled test profiles and mock forms where possible. Record test date, inputs, expected and actual results; do not infer a real submission from an inconclusive response.</p><h1>Chapter 9: Implementation and Maintenance</h1><p>The interface communicates with API services, while workers process queued ingestion and browser tasks. PostgreSQL stores persistent records and Redis coordinates asynchronous work. Deployments require environment-specific configuration, credentials, migrations and monitoring. Maintenance includes updating source adapters and selectors, reviewing failed jobs, dependency updates, backups and restoration checks.</p><h1>Chapter 10: Limitations, Future Scope and Enhancements</h1><p>Current coverage depends on supported integrations and available data. Future work includes additional portal workflows, improved matching, accessibility, clearer analytics and a Java-based matching or integration service. Java prototypes require integration, testing and deployment before they can be treated as operational components.</p><h1>Chapter 11: References and Bibliography</h1><p>1. Project documentation: docs/how-it-works.md and docs/competitive/production-status.md.
2. Node.js documentation: https://nodejs.org/docs/
3. PostgreSQL documentation: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/
4. Playwright documentation: https://playwright.dev/docs/
5. Java documentation: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/</p><h1>Appendix</h1><p>Abbreviations: API — Application Programming Interface; ATS — Applicant Tracking System; DFD — Data Flow Diagram; ER — Entity Relationship; SDLC — Software Development Life Cycle; DSA — Data Structures and Algorithms. Complete the cover details and insert genuine project screenshots before submission.</p></body></html>